{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/app/association.go at exact head e962fa7be247ecad9cf5fdefac2fa2a41bf62818 on request/lane-worktree-identity (pushed), one commit over main 359b211c, clean merge-tree onto current main c0108be8 (tree 62f9f958). This is the implementation report for stage S1, under my promise a4c569b1 on child request ac54fe71 (#22310) of #22168, governed by Hugh's effective adoption ba18cd1a of proposal 7a71886b. It describes this head and nothing else. Nineteen companion artifacts stand beside it, one per changed path, and carry the per-path detail.\n\nWHAT THIS FILE IS. The read-only reverse association: which durable record the implementing commits on each branch tip and each unbranched checkout head claim, and whether authenticated durable evidence corroborates it. Five grades. A source trailer naming a record this workroom holds is claimed; it becomes corroborated only when a standing, effective, unretired artifact statement names that exact commit and reviewguard's own OwnedEdge ties the actor that statement's signature names to the same governing record, or, for self-initiated work, when AdoptedDecision accepts a decision the artifact rests on directly. A trailer naming no record here, or more than one, is unresolved and carries the resolver's typed refusal, candidate list and omitted count verbatim, choosing no winner. A canonical identifier of another genesis is foreign, carried as typed and never resolved locally. Unknown is what every row says when the read did not finish. Corroboration attaches to a commit and never to a branch: a tip past the commit somebody signed for is demoted to claimed, with the signed commit named in corroborated_at. No Git author ident is read, matched or mapped anywhere on this path, so a forged trailer under a copied performer ident stays claimed.\n\nWHAT S1 DELIVERS, BY PATH. internal/gitstore/lineage.go: Store.Lineage, the bounded first-parent read, with LineageResult carrying the commits, the tips cut off by the per-tip limit and the tips this repository does not hold. internal/gitstore/graph.go: the NUL-framed record layout and the empty-history test extracted so that read shares Graph's stride and per-object hash re-verification. internal/reviewguard/binding.go: ownedEdge and adoptedDecision exported as OwnedEdge and AdoptedDecision so this reader uses those rules rather than a private copy; no review-guard decision moves. internal/app/landing.go: MainlineRefs moved here and exported, one list for the landed endpoint and for the lineage boundary. internal/app/landing_graph.go: readLandingRefs split into readRefInventory, the one bounded for-each-ref, and newLandingGraph, a traversal over an already captured inventory. internal/app/checkout_read.go: CheckoutRead, the one captured observation; CaptureCheckouts and captureAround; and AdviseCheckouts, the only way to obtain both answers. internal/app/app.go: the association cache fields, the seven reported WorktreeView fields, the derived checkout root, the containment and duplicate-head facts. internal/app/worktree_landing.go: settledCommitment, the pending-decision scan, and the three added protections. internal/app/association.go: this file. internal/service/ui.go: handleWorktrees captures once and calls AdviseCheckouts. Tests: internal/app/checkout_read_test.go, association_test.go, checkout_reporting_test.go, worktree_index_test.go, internal/gitstore/lineage_test.go, internal/service/worktrees_remote_test.go. Documentation: docs/reference/architecture.md layers 6 and 7, docs/reference/landing-observations.md, and the two notes under notes/.\n\nTHE BOUND AND WHAT REACHING IT DOES, stated once. One worktrees request makes one captured read: the checkout listing taken once, the bounded ref inventory read once, the remote resolved once, one three-second deadline and one 65,536-step budget. Both judgments read that capture and spend that budget, so the bound is on the request rather than on each judgment. Within the read the association carries its own limits: 256 tips, 512 first-parent commits per tip counted per tip, the existing one-mebibyte cap on a commit object, and the existing bounded ref inventory. Reaching any of them, or cancellation, or a ref inventory that is unavailable or above its bound, or a captured tip this repository does not hold, withholds the whole response: the candidate list is empty, every checkout reads unknown for both its classification and its grade with Row, Rows, Approved, LandedInto and RemoteContains cleared, and the association table is incomplete with a stated reason. A repository that genuinely holds no commits stays a complete answer of nothing. The withholding step can only turn an answer into unknown, never the reverse, and it reads no claim; AdviseCheckouts is the only way to obtain both answers, so no caller reaches the pair without it. Unknown never proves a negative, and every one of these directions protects a checkout rather than offering one.\n\nCONDITIONS OF THE S1 REQUEST, ONE BY ONE, AT THIS HEAD.\n\n\"Land the adopted design and the affected architecture layer 6/7 contract paragraphs in the same exact head as layer 1 and its read-only reporting.\" Met: both notes, both architecture paragraph sets, the wire page and the code are in this one commit.\n\n\"Implement claimed/corroborated/unresolved/foreign grading per exact commit, never per whole branch, with copied Git author text plus forged trailers remaining claims.\" Met: grade() keys on commit plus selector, corroborate() matches evidence only against the claim's own governing record, and a corroborated row whose corroborated_at is not the head is demoted.\n\n\"The cache key includes both durable frontier and observed ref inventory digest.\" Met and exceeded: the key is the frontier, a digest of the captured ref inventory, and a digest of the captured checkout listing, because a checkout can be added, removed, renamed or moved to another detached head with no ref and no durable record changing. Only inputs take part; the grades this pass writes back are outputs.\n\n\"Preserve the adopted depth/ref/tip/object/step/deadline bounds, unknown-on-incomplete behavior and discard incomplete cleanup advice.\" Met, as stated above in one place. The adopted note requires the whole batch's advice to be discarded; the whole batch is the response, not the judgment that happened to notice the bound.\n\n\"Read-only association must perform no writes, including when its inputs are unavailable.\" Met: no write exists on this path. TestTheAssociationPassWritesNothing digests the whole repository either side of the pass and compares.\n\n\"Use that one association table for cleanup, duplicate checkout, refless head, containment and pending-decision reporting.\" Met: one captured read answers the whole request and the four reports and the association are computed over it. The two judgments remain two -- the classifier reads no grade and no table, the association reads no classification -- because sharing an observation must not share authority.\n\n\"A live proposal's structural rests_on edge to an artifact protects its candidate while the parent remains unsettled... the Case C counterexample is mandatory. Pair protection controls with a truly eligible candidate.\" Met: the structural edge is what is read, a retired artifact is still a subject, staleness is not settlement, and the Case C fixture carries an eligible candidate that must still be offered.\n\n\"Merge and retirement may print advice, but never delete or mutate refs as a side effect.\" Met by not being changed: no merge or retirement path is touched.\n\n\"No creation, stamping, hook, lane-ref writing or schema change is delivered by S1.\" Met: no gs worktree command, no per-checkout record, no refs/gitseq/lanes ref, no branch field on any durable kind, and the statement schema and fold profile are unchanged.\n\n\"Reuse current inventory, bounded Git scanning, canonical event resolution, landing facts and owned-edge logic; do not add a competing lifecycle or identity store.\" Met: no second identity store exists.\n\n\"Reconcile the note's remaining S2/S3 wording questions.\" Met: the reconciled note's \"Three wording questions, answered\" settles all three and names the stage each binds, holding one item out as an S2 proposal item rather than settling it. Message 4128 is ephemeral chat I could not read; the substance came from the durable stage requests ac54fe71, 0e7c04c8 and 8415ce89, and a reviewer should check the answers there.\n\n\"Independent exact-head review, requester ratification, sealed exact-path succession, push, main CI and owned cleanup.\" Not met at this artifact and not claimable by it: they follow in that order. The head is pushed.\n\n\"S1 and S2 artifacts report their child commitments only.\" This artifact reports the S1 child only. Layers 2, 3 and 4 are undelivered, the parent promise stays open, and nothing here retires the four-layer outcome.\n\nTESTS AT THIS HEAD. internal/app/checkout_read_test.go: TestOneCapturedReadServesBothJudgments, which counts the git subprocesses that actually ran through a shim ahead of git on PATH; TestBothJudgmentsAnswerAboutTheCapturedRefs, which creates a real linked checkout with git worktree add, moves its branch from inside it, and requires exactly one row on the moved branch classified and graded at the captured tip; TestTheSharedBudgetIsSpentByBothJudgments; TestSharingTheReadDoesNotShareAuthority; TestTheSingleJudgmentEntryPointsUseOneCaptureEach; TestExhaustionAfterClassificationWithholdsTheWholeResponse; TestCancellationWithholdsTheWholeResponse; and TestTheSubprocessCountSurvivesPathsWithSpaces. association_test.go: the eleven grading, cache and read-only tests, plus TestAssociationCacheIsKeyedOnTheCapturedCheckoutsToo, TestMoreTipsThanTheLimitReadsIsIncompleteNotTruncated, TestALineagePastThePerTipLimitIsIncompleteNotTruncated and TestAnUnavailableTipIsIncompleteAndNotAnEmptyHistory. checkout_reporting_test.go: the seven cleanup and containment tests, including TestAPendingDecisionProtectsItsCandidateAndNothingElse and TestTheCheckoutInventoryCapRefusesRatherThanTruncating. lineage_test.go: three. worktrees_remote_test.go: TestWorktreesResponseCarriesTheCheckoutAssociation. worktree_index_test.go keeps the exhaustive differential oracle, which computes the three added protections independently of the bounded index.\n\nCONTROLS, twenty-five, all run against this tree and all killed; none survived. Each removes or inverts one guard in a scratch copy of the worktree, outside the repository. m1 to m4 cover exact-commit evidence, the governing-record match, foreign carry and per-commit corroboration; m5 to m9 the pending-decision and retired-artifact protections, the refless and containment cases and duplicate reporting; m10 to m12 the cache key, the whole-batch discard and stale-is-not-settled; m13 to m15 object re-verification, the first-parent walk and the inventory cap; m16 and m17 ambiguity refusal and the membership check; m18 to m20 the checkout digest, the exhaustion signal and the unheld tip; m21 and m22 the second ref read and the separate budget; m23 the whole-response discard; m24 the subprocess shim's quoting; m25 the linked-checkout listing. The full table is attached as mutants-s1.txt. Two of them are worth naming. m24 first survived, because the spaced-directory test skipped when the temporary directory was not honoured; a control that can skip proves nothing, so it now fails loudly instead, and the mutant dies. m25 exists because the captured-ref test had been looping over checkouts it never created, so its assertion was vacuous.\n\nGATES, eight, all rc=0 and all fresh at this head; nothing is carried. Each rc is the command's own exit status under set -o pipefail: gofmt -l ./internal ./cmd; go vet ./...; go build ./...; go test -count=1 over internal/app, internal/gitstore, internal/service, internal/reviewguard and internal/statusview; go test -race -count=1 ./internal/app/; go test -count=1 ./internal/docset/..., green on its first run; go test -count=1 ./...; git diff --check. The log is attached as gates-s1-v5.txt.\n\nWHAT S1 DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO. No configured checkout root: the adopted note names one and no such key exists in this source, so the root is derived as the directory holding the served checkout, which can only over-protect, and that is stated where it is used and in both documentation pages. No cleanup advice printed by gs merge or request retirement: that is presentation on two CLI commands over the table this head builds, and it is not layer 1. Git environment bounding is not unified here; the commit read inherits internal/gitstore's bounding exactly as Graph does, and unifying it is the separate request the architecture page names. ui/src/lib/api.ts is not extended: no browser code reads the association yet, the wireparity gate does not cover WorktreeView, and this is a stated gap rather than one a gate hid. And no gs worktree command, per-checkout record, refs/gitseq/lanes ref, branch field, schema change or fold profile advance: those are S2 and S3.\n\nREVIEW HISTORY, three verdicts, all from codex, all changes-requested, all ratified, and all on this same S1 promise. (1) git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:19b37887a299a3e1a8dda2ec301e71e642e775a6 on 0d1c7e11, ratified 6803776f: the association cache omitted the captured checkout inputs; bounded stops reported a complete-looking short answer; an unheld tip read as an empty history and the annotation step cleared unknown to blank. (2) git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:91c50bf9d8ef0c48cb0f855fe0324a30434f14d8 on 643ab9c5, ratified 2d590023: the assigned one-table condition was not delivered, because each judgment opened its own deadline, budget and ref read. (3) git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:5b80e91ec3534307bd52392f4e8f92ab4e69ef86 on 557ef676, ratified ffd7ffc6: exhaustion after classification still published the candidate list; the subprocess shim interpolated unquoted paths and reported zero reads under a spaced directory; the captured-ref test never created its linked checkout; five artifacts were flagged describes_superseded_world through a checkpoint basis; and this report had accumulated round-by-round narrative that no longer matched its own head. Each is answered at this head, in the code, the tests, the controls, the bases and this text.\n\nTWO THINGS TO SAY PLAINLY. First, the provenance repair: the artifacts of this set rest only on live, in-target, unflagged predecessors -- for internal/app/app.go and docs/reference/architecture.md, the successors published by the merge at c0108be8 -- and on no checkpoint artifact and no artifact of an earlier head of this branch. That was asserted mechanically against a fresh projection rather than by choosing a convenient current identifier. Second, the reviewer's whole-batch probe: it passes here when its two judgment calls are routed through AdviseCheckouts, as the endpoint does. Its raw-locals form cannot pass unchanged, because a []string already returned to a caller cannot be emptied retroactively; the discard has to be the step that produces the pair. That is a difference in where the probe calls, not in what it proves.\n\nNO LIVE SERVICE WAS TOUCHED. Every fixture is a temporary repository built by internal/testgit. No resident was restarted, no hook installed, no checkout or branch removed, no ref written or deleted, and no key operated. New path in the merge target. The artifact at 557ef676 is the outside-target predecessor this head replaces; it is retired separately with this artifact as its successor.","body":{"branch":"request/lane-worktree-identity","commit":"e962fa7be247ecad9cf5fdefac2fa2a41bf62818","path":"internal/app/association.go","stale_bases":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:a4c569b1cddfb0db72f9239488851f19a291906f, git:sha